course: dictionary
lesson: 6738
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one to use. Olympic can mean about the Olympic Games, the biggest international sports competition. It can also describe something very big or of top standard. «The club built an Olympic-size pool, 50 metres long.» The size clue tells you Olympic means big and of top standard here, not about the Games.
explain: Look at the words around it and ask what the sentence is about: sport, history or size? «The first Olympic Games were held in ancient Greece.» Here the clues are first, Games and ancient Greece, so Olympic means about the Games.
explain: Then check your choice. Swap your meaning into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have probably chosen well. If it sounds wrong, read the sentence again and look for more clues.
fact: Olympic (adjective, usually with a capital O) means about the Olympic Games.
fact: The Olympics (noun) is the biggest international sports competition, held every four years in summer and winter versions.
fact: «Olympic» can also mean very big or of top standard, as in «an Olympic-size pool», which is 50 metres long.
fact: The first Olympic Games were held in ancient Greece at Olympia.
fact: The Paralympic Games are for disabled athletes.
exercise: «She trained every day to become an Olympic champion.» What does Olympic mean here? | Found in old books | Very quiet and calm | *Connected with the Olympic Games, the biggest sports competition | Made of gold
exercise: «The club built an Olympic-size pool, 50 metres long.» What does Olympic-size mean? | Full of fish | Hot and sunny | Small and shallow | *Very big and of top standard
exercise: Copy the noun from this sentence, the name of the event: «The Olympics are held every four years.» | years | *Olympics | every | four
test: «The first Olympic Games were held in ancient Greece at Olympia.» Where were the first Games held? | Milan in Italy | *Olympia in ancient Greece | London in England | Los Angeles in America
test: «The Olympic flag has five rings.» What do the rings stand for? | Five rivers | *The continents | The five best athletes | The number of medals won
exam: «He won an Olympic medal at the winter Games.» What does Olympic mean in this sentence? | Very big | Very old and broken | *About the Olympic Games | Made in Italy
